        "## 4. Model Design and Justification\n",
        "\n",
        "The model choice should match the structure of the problem. In this project, the inputs are time-ordered financial and macroeconomic variables, and the outputs are two future quantities: the **30-trading-day ahead gold return** and the **21-trading-day ahead realized volatility**. Because both targets depend on how market conditions evolve through time, the model must be able to use sequential information rather than treat each row as an isolated observation.\n",
        "\n",
        "For that reason, the main model in this project is a **sequence model** built on a rolling input window. At each prediction date, the model receives the most recent block of observations for gold prices and the external variables, and it uses that history to estimate the two future targets.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4.1 Input Structure\n",
        "\n",
        "Each training example is built from a fixed window of past observations. A natural choice is a **60-day lookback window**, although this can later be varied during tuning.\n",
        "\n",
        "For each day in the input window, the model receives variables such as:\n",
        "\n",
        "- gold open, high, low, close, and volume  \n",
        "- gold returns and rolling volatility  \n",
        "- interest-rate variables  \n",
        "- inflation expectations  \n",
        "- dollar index  \n",
        "- market uncertainty indicators  \n",
        "- oil prices  \n",
        "- geopolitical risk and policy uncertainty measures  \n",
        "\n",
        "This structure allows the model to use both price dynamics and the broader market environment at the same time.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4.2 Main Architecture: LSTM-Based Multi-Output Model\n",
        "\n",
        "The central model is an **LSTM network** followed by fully connected layers and two separate output heads.\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple description of the architecture is:\n",
        "\n",
        "- an input sequence of the last 60 trading days  \n",
        "- one or two LSTM layers to summarize the recent history  \n",
        "- a dense hidden layer to refine the learned representation  \n",
        "- one output head for future return  \n",
        "- one output head for future volatility  \n",
        "\n",
        "This design is appropriate for these possible reasons\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "First, an LSTM is well suited to ordered data where recent values, medium-term patterns, and changes in regime may all matter. Gold prices do not move independently from one day to the next. The same is true for volatility, which often appears in clusters rather than in isolated jumps. An LSTM is able to process that kind of structure more naturally than a plain feedforward network.\n",
        "\n",
        "Second, the model is built with **two outputs** rather than two completely separate models. This is useful because return and volatility are not the same quantity, but they are related. Both are influenced by the same market state: rates, uncertainty, momentum, and geopolitical stress. A shared sequence encoder lets the model learn a common representation of current market conditions, while the two output heads allow each target to keep its own final mapping.\n",
        "\n",
        "Third, this architecture remains small enough to train and explain clearly. It is strong enough for the project without becoming unnecessarily complicated.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4.3 Justification  for  LSTM Is the Main Model\n",
        "\n",
        "Among the architectures covered in class, the LSTM is the most natural starting point for this task.\n",
        "\n",
        "A multilayer perceptron can use engineered lag features, but it does not directly process temporal order. That makes it useful as a baseline, but not as the main design.\n",
        "\n",
        "A Transformer can also be used for sequential data, and it will be included later as a comparison model. Still, for a project of this size, an LSTM gives a better balance between interpretability, training stability, and sequence handling. It also fits the type of signal present in financial time series, where the model must capture short-term dependence, trend shifts, and changes in volatility.\n",
        "\n",
        "For these reasons, the LSTM serves as the main architecture, while the MLP and Transformer are used as reference points in the experimental section.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4.4 Baseline Model: MLP\n",
        "\n",
        "A **multilayer perceptron** is included as a baseline. Its inputs are not raw sequences but engineered summary features, such as lagged returns, moving-average gaps, rolling volatility, and lagged macroeconomic variables.\n",
        "\n",
