        "\n",
        "### 5.1 Data Split\n",
        "\n",
        "This project works with a time series, so the data is split by date order. A shuffled split would place future observations inside earlier training periods, which would distort the forecasting task.\n",
        "\n",
        "The dataset is divided into three parts.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **training set** for fitting model parameters  \n",
        "- **validation set** for tuning hyperparameters and choosing the model  \n",
        "- **test set** for the final out-of-sample check  \n",
        "\n",
        "The newest part of the sample is held back for testing. This makes the final results closer to a real forecasting exercise.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.2 Main Model Comparison\n",
        "\n",
        "The project compares three model types.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **MLP**, used as a baseline with engineered lag features  \n",
        "- **LSTM**, used as the main sequence model  \n",
        "- **Transformer encoder**, used as another sequence-based model  \n",
        "\n",
        "The comparison is useful because each model reads the data in a different way.\n",
        "\n",
        "The MLP works with prepared summary features\t\tand has no direct sequence memory.  \n",
        "The LSTM reads recent observations in order\t\tand is built for time-ordered inputs.  \n",
        "The Transformer encoder tests whether attention across the lookback window improves the forecast.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.3 Common Training Setup\n",
        "\n",
        "The models follow the same training rules where possible.\n",
        "\n",
        "- same train, validation, and test periods  \n",
        "- same target definitions  \n",
        "- same scaling procedure  \n",
        "- same evaluation metrics  \n",
        "- same early stopping rule  \n",
        "- fixed random seeds  \n",
        "\n",
        "This setup makes the comparison easier to read. Performance gaps can then be linked more fairly to model structure\t\trather than changes in the experiment.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.4 Prediction Targets\n",
        "\n",
        "Each experiment studies two targets.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **30-trading-day ahead log return**\n",
        "- **21-trading-day ahead realized volatility**\n",
        "\n",
        "These targets describe separate parts of the gold market. The return target measures future price movement. The volatility target measures market turbulence over the next period.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.5 Evaluation Metrics\n",
        "\n",
        "For the return target, the main measures are:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **RMSE**\n",
        "- **MAE**d\n",
        "- **directional accuracy**\n",
        "\n",
        "For the volatility target, the main measures are:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **RMSE**\n",
        "- **MAE**\n",
        "- **correlation between predicted and actual volatility**\n",
        "d\n",
        "These metrics check both forecast error\t\tand practical value. A model can have a small average error yet still miss direction or volatility changes.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.6 Core Experiments\n",
        "\n",
        "The experimental section contains the comparisons below.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Experiment 1: Baseline againsdt sequence models\n",
        "\n",
        "The first experiment compares the MLP, LSTM, and Transformer on the same prediction task.  \n",
        "The goal is to see whether sequence-aware models give a meaningful gain over the simpler baseline.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Experiment 2: Price-only inputs against full inputs\n",
        "\n",
        "The second experiment compares two input settings.\n",
        "\n",
        "- models trained only with gold-based variables  \n",
        "- models trained with gold variables plus macroeconomic and geopolitical variables  \n",
        "\n",
        "This test checks whether outside variables add real forecasting value.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Experiment 3: Input window length\n",
        "\n",
        "The third experiment compares several lookback windows.\n",
        "\n",
        "- 30 trading days  \n",
        "- 60 trading days  \n",
        "- 120 trading days  \n",
        "\n",
        "This helps measure how much past information the model needs for a stable forecast.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Experiment 4: Output structure\n",
