        "## 7. Evaluation and Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "The final evaluation used a chronological split. After preprocessing and target construction, the usable sample contained **6,270 records**, covering **2001-01-24 to 2026-01-16**. The data was divided into three parts.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **training set** 4,389 records, from 2001-01-24 to 2018-07-26  \n",
        "- **validation set** 940 records, from 2018-07-27 to 2022-04-20  \n",
        "- **test set** 941 records, from 2022-04-21 to 2026-01-16  \n",
        "\n",
        "This split keeps the test period outside model fitting\t\tand preserves the order of the time series.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 7.1 Main Results\n",
        "\n",
        "The strongest setting inside each model family gave the results below.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Model | Validation loss | Test RMSE return | Test MAE return | Test directional accuracy | Test RMSE volatility | Test MAE volatility | Test volatility correlation |\n",
        "|------|------:|------:|------:|------:|------:|------:|------:|\n",
        "| MLP | 0.162934 | 0.101792 | 0.080263 | 0.314559 | 0.013151 | 0.009951 | 0.973155 |\n",
        "| LSTM | 0.176484 | 0.077547 | 0.061947 | 0.294182 | 0.029859 | 0.024584 | 0.889228 |\n",
        "| Transformer | 0.177348 | 0.084901 | 0.068666 | 0.294182 | 0.029202 | 0.022719 | 0.932445 |\n",
        "\n",
        "Using the joint validation loss, the **MLP** was chosen as the best overall model. This result needs a careful reading\t\tsince the model did better on one target than the other.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 7.2 Return Forecasting\n",
        "\n",
        "For the **30-day ahead return**, the best test results came from the **LSTM with a 30-day lookback window**. It reached the lowest test RMSE, **0.077547**, and the lowest test MAE, **0.061947**, among the selected models.\n",
        "\n",
        "The Transformer also reduced return error compared with the MLP, although it stayed behind the LSTM. The MLP won the joint validation loss\t\tyet gave the weakest return forecast on the test set.\n",
        "\n",
        "This suggests that the sequence models used recent ordering better when predicting future returns. Return behavior depends on more than lag summaries. The order of recent movements\t\tand their short-run interactions seem to matter.\n",
        "\n",
        "Directional accuracy was weak across all three models in the test period. Every value stayed below **0.32**, which means the models struggled to predict the sign of the future return. In practical terms, they captured part of the return size\t\twithout giving dependable direction calls.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 7.3 Volatility Forecasting\n",
        "\n",
        "For the **21-day ahead realized volatility**, the results changed sharply.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **MLP** gave the stronfgest test performance.\n",
        "\n",
        "- lowest volatility RMSE  \n",
        "- lowest volatility MAE  \n",
        "- highest correlation between predicted and realized volatility  \n",
        "\n",
        "Its volatility correlation reached **0.973155**, vabove the LSTM at **0.889228** and the Transformer at **0.932445**.\n",
        "\n",
        "This shows that volatility was easier to forecast vthan returns. A simpler feedforward model already captured much of the volatility signal. This result makes sense for financial data\t\tsince volatility often has stronger persistence than returns.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 7.4 What the Training Curves Show\n",
        "\n",
        "The training curves help explain the model behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the **MLP**, training and validation losses remain close. The validation curve moves inside a narrow band\t\tand shows a steadier fit under the chosen loss.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the **LSTM**, training loss keeps falling after the validation loss reaches its best point. The gap between both curves grows, which points to overfitting.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **Transformer** follows a related  pattern. Training loss keeps dropping, then validation loss stops improving and starts moving upward. This suggests that the model begins to fit the training sample too closely.\n",
        "\n",
        "The sequence models produced stronger return error on the test set\t\tyet their validation curves were less steady during training.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 7.5 Interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "The results point to a divided outcome across  the two targets.\n",
        "\n",
        "- the **MLP** was strongest for **volatility**\n",
        "- the **LSTM** was strongest for **return error**\n",
        "- the **Transformer** stayed competitive without leading either target\n",
        "\n",
        "This matters because the project has **two targets** . When the two targets are combined into a joint validation loss, the MLP’s strong volatility forecast carries enough weight to offset its weaker return forecast. That explains why it was selected under the current training objective.\n",
        "\n",
        "The best choice depends on the main question.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the **joint two-target task**, the **MLP** is the stronger choice under the current selection rule.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the **return target alone**, the **LSTM** gives  the stronger out-of-sample result.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 7.6 Error Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "Several patterns stand out.\n",
        "\n",
        "The low **directional accuracy** on the test set  suggests that the most recent period was harder to predict than the validation period. The test sample begins in 2022\t\tand includes strong macroeconomic and geopolitical shocks, which may have changed the behavior of the series.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **30-day lookback** performed better than the **60-day lookback** for the LSTM. This suggests that return forecasting benefited more from recent information than from a longer window.\n",
        "\n",
        "The Transformer failed to improve over the LSTM in this setting. Possible reasons include sample size, model size, limited tuning , or a feature set that did not need a more flexible attention structure.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 7.7 Final Takeaway\n",
        "\n",
        "The key result is that the task separates into two  parts.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Return forecasting** remained difficult, with the **LSTM** giving the best result among the tested models.\n",
        "- **Volatility forecasting** was steadier, with the **MLP** giving the best result by a wide margin.\n",
        "\n",
        "The project supports two conclusions.\n",
        "\n",
        "1. For the **joint prediction task**, the **MLP** is the  preferred model under the current validation rule.\n",
        "2. For the **return target alone**, the **LSTM with a 30-day lookback** is the stronger model.\n",
